SERVER_PREFIX=claude

# Provider: hetzner (default), aws, gcp, azure, kubernetes or compose (gcp and azure are only tested against simulated interfaces; a run in a real account is not documented). The settings below have the defaults of the provider.
# PROVIDER=hetzner

# Hetzner: https://www.hetzner.com/de/cloud/regular-performance/  cpx02 cpx22 cpx32 cpx42 cpx52 cpx62
#   SERVER_TYPE=cpx32  SERVER_LOCATION=fsn1  SERVER_IMAGE=ubuntu-26.04
# AWS: instance type, region (or zone, e.g. eu-central-1b) and ubuntu-<version> or an AMI ID
#   SERVER_TYPE=t3.large  SERVER_LOCATION=eu-central-1  SERVER_IMAGE=ubuntu-24.04
#   AWS_PROFILE, AWS_SUBNET_ID, AWS_VPC_ID and AWS_ROOT_GB (size of the root disk, default 50) are optional;
#   AWS_TAGS=owner=me,project=x adds tags to the instance and its volume at creation (no secrets);
#   AWS_TAGS_SHARED=project=x does the same for the key pair and security group (shared by all machines: no ttl);
#   the credentials come from the AWS credentials chain (aws login, SSO, keys)
# GCP: SERVER_TYPE=e2-standard-2  SERVER_LOCATION=europe-west3-b  (a zone); GCP_PROJECT is required;
#   GCP_NETWORK, GCP_SUBNET (a custom-mode network) and GCP_ROOT_GB are optional
# Azure: SERVER_TYPE=Standard_D2s_v5  SERVER_LOCATION=germanywestcentral; AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID is required;
#   AZURE_RESOURCE_GROUP, AZURE_SUBNET_ID and AZURE_ROOT_GB are optional
# Kubernetes (also a local k3d cluster): a machine is a pod. Put these in machines/<machine>.env.local or in a group file,
#   not here (they are per machine, and DNS_PROVIDER=none is needed). viper image build [--k3d CLUSTER] makes the image.
#   PROVIDER=kubernetes  DNS_PROVIDER=none
#   SERVER_LOCATION=k3d-viper   (the context of your kubeconfig; no default, viper never takes the current one)
#   K8S_IMAGE=<name that viper image build printed>   (optional: viper takes the published image of its own
#     version, ghcr.io/pt9912/viper-machine:<its version>)
#   SERVER_TYPE=2x4   (<CPUs>x<GiB>)  K8S_NAMESPACE=viper  K8S_STORAGE_CLASS, K8S_DISK_GB=20, K8S_BREW_GB=10 are optional
#   K8S_DOCKER=dind|remote|none (Docker on the machine; dind is a privileged container), K8S_IMAGE_PULL_SECRET for a registry login
#   k3d only (SERVER_LOCATION=k3d-<cluster>): K3D_REGISTRY=viper-registry makes viper up create a k3d registry and put the
#   image into it, so that every node pulls it from there; K3D_CLUSTER_CREATE=1 lets viper up create a missing cluster with it
#   (K3D_AGENTS=2 agent nodes). Like all settings they can also be exported in the shell.
# Docker Compose: a machine is a container on the Docker host of this computer (no cloud account, no cluster).
#   PROVIDER=compose  DNS_PROVIDER=none
#   SERVER_LOCATION=default   (the Docker context; "default" is the local daemon; no default, DOCKER_HOST must not be set)
#   COMPOSE_IMAGE=<name that viper image build printed>   (the same default as K8S_IMAGE)
#   SERVER_TYPE=2x4   COMPOSE_DOCKER=dind|remote|none (dind is a privileged container: say so on purpose; remote needs COMPOSE_DOCKER_HOST)
# SERVER_TYPE=
# SERVER_LOCATION=
# SERVER_IMAGE=
# DNS name of the machines: cloudflare (default) makes an A record <SERVER_PREFIX>-<machine>.<DNS_DOMAIN>
# in a Cloudflare zone (needs DNS_DOMAIN and the Cloudflare credentials below). none makes no record:
# no domain and no Cloudflare account are needed; connect with viper ssh <machine> (AWS also shows the
# name the cloud gives the server, which changes with its address).
# DNS_PROVIDER=cloudflare
DNS_DOMAIN=
SSH_KEY="$HOME/.ssh/hetzner-build"
# SSH from anywhere (iPad / mobile). Protected by public-key-only sshd configuration.
# Narrow this to your networks if possible.
SSH_ALLOWED_CIDRS=0.0.0.0/0,::/0

# Optional: public SSH key from iPad, one OpenSSH public key per file
IPAD_SSH_PUBLIC_KEY_FILE=

# Install Homebrew (brew) on the server: 1 (default) or 0 (saves 1-2 minutes per new server).
# Values in these files take precedence over variables exported in the shell.
# VIPER_HOMEBREW=1
# Homebrew, separated by spaces, applied in this order: taps (extra package sources), trust
# (brew trust for third-party taps or formulae), formulae (tools). A tap is "owner/repo" (on
# GitHub) or "owner/repo=URL". Trust: "owner/repo" = the whole tap, "owner/repo/name" = one
# formula, or "cask:…" / "command:…". A formula is "name" or "owner/repo/name".
# Installing owner/repo/name trusts that one formula already; VIPER_BREW_TRUST is for
# formulae installed by short name, casks, external commands, or a whole tap.
# VIPER_BREWFILE=1 also applies a Brewfile of the cloned repository (brew bundle). It is Ruby
# code from the repository and its trusted: entries change Homebrew's trust store.
# Lists: entries separated by spaces or line breaks, the whole value in double quotes.
# VIPER_BREW_TAPS="hashicorp/tap"
# VIPER_BREW_TRUST=""
# VIPER_BREWFILE=0
# VIPER_BREW_PACKAGES="ripgrep fd hashicorp/tap/terraform"

# Optional: shell commands run on the server in the cloned repository after the clone and
# Homebrew, once per clone (login shell as builder; they stop at the first failing command; after
# a failure the next viper up tries again). Several lines: the whole value in double quotes.
# Put the value in single quotes: $VARIABLES then reach the server unchanged (in double quotes
# they are expanded here, on your computer). Needs a repository. Log on the server: ~/.config/viper/post-clone.log
# VIPER_POST_CLONE='npm ci
# make setup'
# If they fail: retry (default, a warning; the next viper up tries again), ignore (a warning,
# never tried again) or abort (viper up fails, the server stays; the next viper up tries again).
# VIPER_POST_CLONE_ON_ERROR=retry

# Optional: swap file on the server (a safety net against the OOM killer, off by default):
# a factor of the server's RAM (1 = as much as RAM, 0.5 = half; 0.1 to 4) or a size (4G, 512M).
# VIPER_SWAP=1

# Directory on the server that holds the cloned repositories (deleted with the server). Default /home/builder/work; a
# container accepts only a directory below /home/builder/.
# WORKSPACE_DIR=/home/builder/work

# Credentials (or export them in your shell instead; never commit .env.local)
# Hetzner: HCLOUD_TOKEN. AWS: none here, the AWS credentials chain (aws login) is used. GCP: GCP_PROJECT
# and the application default credentials (gcloud auth application-default login). Azure:
# AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID and az login.
# HCLOUD_TOKEN=
# GCP_PROJECT=
# AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID=
# Cloudflare, only with DNS_PROVIDER=cloudflare:
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
# CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=

# Optional: author of git commits made on the server. Without them, VIPER_GH_TOKEN (below)
# supplies the GitHub account's name and noreply address; with neither, commits there fail.
# VIPER_GIT_USER_NAME="Your Name"
# VIPER_GIT_USER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Optional: code-server (VS Code in the browser) on the server, on 127.0.0.1:8080 only; reach it
# with an SSH tunnel: ssh -L 8080:127.0.0.1:8080 hetzner-<server>. The password needs 12+
# characters without spaces; quote it if it has special characters.
# VIPER_CODE_SERVER=1
# VIPER_CODE_SERVER_PASSWORD=

# Optional: GitHub personal access token for gh and git over HTTPS on the server.
# Prefer a fine-grained, repo-scoped, short-lived token per machine (machines/<machine>.env.local).
# Deliberately not GH_TOKEN: a token exported for your local gh is never copied to a server.
# VIPER_GH_TOKEN=
# VIPER_GH_HOST=github.com

# Optional: Claude Code login on the server without /login. A long-lived token for your
# Claude subscription (Pro, Max, Team, Enterprise), created once with `claude setup-token`
# (valid for one year; usage counts against the subscription, not API credits).
# Deliberately not CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN: a token exported locally is never copied.
# VIPER_CLAUDE_TOKEN=

# Optional instead: another model behind an Anthropic-compatible API (Ollama's cloud, a vLLM
# server, a gateway). Usage is billed by that provider. Not together with VIPER_CLAUDE_TOKEN.
# Ollama cloud: base URL https://ollama.com, token = Ollama API key (ollama.com/settings/keys)
# VIPER_ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://ollama.com
# VIPER_ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
# VIPER_CLAUDE_MODEL=
# Context window Claude Code should assume for that model (tokens), e.g. 1048576
# VIPER_CLAUDE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=

# NATS group (optional, off by default; Hetzner only for now): this machine joins the NATS group
# NATS_GROUP; the first machine of a group runs the server, the others are clients. Group and
# machine name: 1 to 31 characters, lowercase letters, digits and hyphens.
# NATS_ENABLED=true
# NATS_GROUP=entwicklung
# NATS_PORT=4222
# MCP server for Claude Code on the machine (needs NATS_ENABLED): tools to talk to the other machines.
# NATS_MCP_PUSH_FROM: machines whose messages are pushed into a session started by you with
# claude --dangerously-load-development-channels server:nats (viper never starts it); empty: none.
# NATS_MCP_TOPICS: topics every MCP server subscribes to at its start (like the tool subscribe, but
# fixed: unsubscribe refuses them); comma separated, at most 16. Put them in groups/<group>.env to
# have them on all machines of the group.
# NATS_MCP_AUTOALLOW: tools allowed without asking: off (default), read (peers, inbox, topics) or all (all also allows fetch).
# NATS_OBJECTS: files (images, PDFs, ZIP files) in messages, kept by the NATS server of the group (needs NATS_MCP=true).
# Put it, and NATS_OBJECTS_MAX (MiB), NATS_OBJECTS_FILE_MAX (MiB) and NATS_OBJECTS_TTL (days), in the group file
# groups/<group>.env: it is fixed when the server of the group is made. See the manual.
# NATS_MCP_BINARY: install this file instead of the one built into viper (a viper from plain go build).
# NATS_MCP=true
# NATS_MCP_PUSH_FROM=
# NATS_MCP_TOPICS=
# NATS_MCP_AUTOALLOW=off
# Several machines together: groups/<group>.env names them (GROUP_MACHINES=alpha,beta) and holds the
# settings for all of them; viper up/down/check --group <group> works on all at once. See
# groups/example.env.example (viper setup creates it).
